"Connects a business goal to relevant actors, desired behavior changes and possible deliverables that could support those changes.\n\nUse when: Challenge whether proposed scope can contribute to an objective. Generate alternative deliverables for the same behavioral impact.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need a delivery schedule or a complete causal proof of the intervention.\n\nVisual grammar: Four levels distinguish why, who, how and what. Branches connect goals to actors, impacts and deliverables. Assumption notes qualify the reasoning behind each contribution.\n\nBuild: State a concrete goal and identify actors who affect it. Describe the behavior changes those actors would need to make. Generate deliverables that could support those impacts and test the links.\n\nCheck: Impacts describe actor behavior rather than team outputs. Deliverables connect to a plausible impact. Alternatives remain visible instead of implying every leaf must be built.\n\nPitfalls: Skipping directly from a broad goal to a feature list. Treating a plausible branch as evidence that the outcome will occur.\n\nExample: A museum seeks repeat visits, identifies local families, targets discovery of new events and explores a calendar and neighborhood partnerships.\n\nAlternatives: Opportunity solution tree, Theory of change."
